<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Diagnosis and severity (J44.9). COPD is confirmed: the post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC of 0.58 is below the GOLD fixed ratio of 0.70 and below the lower limit of normal, with only a small bronchodilator response. Following the 1976 ATS statement, the results were interpreted with the race-neutral GLI Global equations: FEV1 61% of predicted, GOLD grade 2 (moderate). Race-specific equations for African American men predict lower normal values and would have put his FEV1 near 70%, making his disease look milder than it is. The normal alpha-1 antitrypsin excludes deficiency. [S]</item><item>Maintenance treatment (J44.9). With a CAT of 18, mMRC 2 and no exacerbations in the past year he is in GOLD group B, for which GOLD 1978 recommends a LAMA with a LABA in one inhaler. An inhaled corticosteroid is not indicated: his eosinophils are 0.08 x 10^3/uL and he has had no exacerbations, so it would add the risk of pneumonia without benefit. A once-daily dry powder inhaler suits him; his inspiratory effort is strong, and he learned the technique on a placebo device. Treatment should be reviewed for symptoms and exacerbations at each visit; if exacerbations recur despite good technique, the eosinophil count guides the next step. [S]</item><item>Oxygen (J44.9). Pulse oximetry can overestimate arterial saturation in people with darker skin, so a room-air arterial blood gas was done. His PaO2 of 70 mm Hg is well above the long-term oxygen threshold of 55 mm Hg (60 with cor pulmonale or polycythemia), and his lowest walking saturation was 90 percent. Long-term oxygen did not help people with moderate desaturation in the LOTT trial, and he does not have the severe exertional hypoxemia for which the ATS suggests ambulatory oxygen. He needs no oxygen now; this will be reassessed if his saturations fall. [S]</item><item>Rehabilitation and lung cancer screening (J44.9). He is a good candidate for pulmonary rehabilitation (mMRC 2, CAT 18): twice a week for about ten weeks, with walk tests at entry and exit. He is eligible for lung cancer screening at 73 with about 30 pack-years, having quit eleven years ago, and is fit for treatment; after a shared decision he wants a low-dose CT. A CT may also show emphysema and coronary calcium, which would bear on his cardiovascular care. [S]</item><item>Action plan and follow-up (J44.9). A spoken and written action plan: the daily inhaler, the reliever, a same-day call for more breathlessness or a change in sputum color or amount, and 911 for breathlessness at rest, chest pain or confusion; influenza, RSV and COVID-19 vaccines in the autumn, PCV20 and Tdap already given; staying smoke-free. Spirometry yearly. Heart disease is common in COPD; the normal ECG and NT-proBNP are reassuring, and the statin is appropriate. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Whitaker-Osei&apos;s intake note and results: eosinophils 0.08 x 10^3/uL, alpha-1 antitrypsin 142 mg/dL, NT-proBNP 92 pg/mL, a normal ECG, hemoglobin 15.2 g/dL. Today: spirometry meeting ATS/ERS quality standards, FEV1 1.92 L before and 2.02 L after 400 micrograms of albuterol, FVC 3.48 L, post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC 0.58, FEV1 61% of the GLI Global predicted value; room-air arterial blood gas pH 7.42, PaCO2 40 mm Hg, PaO2 70 mm Hg; six-minute walk 360 m with a lowest saturation of 90 percent; CAT 18, mMRC 2. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

